  "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-2x+1,$ show that $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist {{field-1}} constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & \\le {{field-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & <{{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking {{field-7}} and {{field-8}} satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$",
